## 1. Curated Literature Records (12 Papers)

All 12 records below have been programmatically verified against Crossref, PubMed, and Europe PMC. None are currently cited in [`manuscript/references.bib`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/manuscript/references.bib). Full texts for all 11 open-access items (PDF, XML, or complete HTML) have been downloaded to the assigned folder; the one closed-access classic ([Yang & Nielsen 2000](https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.molbev.a026236)) is recorded as paywalled.

### 1. Eberhardt et al. (2012) — AntiFam: Spurious ORF Identification
- **Title:** *AntiFam: a tool to help identify spurious ORFs in protein annotation*
- **Authors & Year:** Ruth Y. Eberhardt, Daniel H. Haft, Mhairi Punta, Matthew Martin, Claire O'Donovan, Alex Bateman (2012)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/database/bas003`](https://doi.org/10.1093/database/bas003)
- **Publisher URL:** [Oxford Database `10.1093/database/bas003`](https://doi.org/10.1093/database/bas003)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC3308159](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC3308159/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`eberhardt_2012_antifam.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/eberhardt_2012_antifam.fulltext.xml) (44.5 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Automated prokaryotic gene callers emit spurious ORFs translated from non-coding RNA (rRNA, tRNA, tmRNA), antisense shadow frames, and vector fragments. Identifies 1,770 spurious proteins in UniProtKB.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** P4's 753M Pyrodigal calls (`-p meta --no-stop-codon`) were not screened against AntiFam or non-coding RNA coordinates. Spurious non-coding translations would contaminate the dark catalogue without P4's multi-sample recurrence filter ($\ge 3$ samples, $\ge 2$ sites) and +1 frameshift selection test.

### 2. Miravet-Verde et al. (2019) — The smORF Discovery Trade-off in Bacteria
- **Title:** *Unraveling the hidden universe of small proteins in bacterial genomes*
- **Authors & Year:** Roberto Miravet-Verde, Teresa Ferrar, Gabriel Espadas-García, et al. (2019)
- **DOI:** [`10.15252/msb.20188290`](https://doi.org/10.15252/msb.20188290)
- **Publisher URL:** [Molecular Systems Biology `10.15252/msb.20188290`](https://doi.org/10.15252/msb.20188290)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC6385055](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC6385055/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`miravet-verde_2019_small-proteins.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/miravet-verde_2019_small-proteins.fulltext.xml) (199.8 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No (Samuel Miravet-Verde is cited only on Maeke et al. 2025; this foundational 2019 MSB paper is absent).
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Ab initio callers (Prodigal/Glimmer) heavily penalize ORFs <100 aa (250–300 bp) to avoid chance stop-to-stop frames, missing up to 70% of true small proteins. Proves genuine smORFs evolve under purifying selection ($Ka/Ks < 1$).
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Explains why 76% of P4's dark representatives are <100 aa. UniRef50 is depleted of smORFs due to historical caller cutoffs; P4's "darkness" is substantially an ascertainment boundary of database reference deposition rather than deep phylogenetic divergence.

### 3. Durrant & Bhatt (2021) — smORFinder & Prodigal smORF Fragility
- **Title:** *Automated Prediction and Annotation of Small Open Reading Frames in Microbial Genomes*
- **Authors & Year:** Matthew G. Durrant and Ami S. Bhatt (2021)
- **DOI:** [`10.1016/j.chom.2020.11.002`](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chom.2020.11.002)
- **Publisher URL:** [Cell Host & Microbe `10.1016/j.chom.2020.11.002`](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.chom.2020.11.002)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [PubMed Central PMC7856248](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7856248/)
- **Local File:** [`durrant_2020_smorfinder.fulltext.html`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/durrant_2020_smorfinder.fulltext.html) (225.5 KB HTML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Deep neural network filtering pruned ~70% of raw Prodigal core smORF predictions (692 down to 213). Identified that programmed translational frameshifts (e.g., *prfB* / RF2) cause Prodigal to emit spurious truncated smORF calls.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Raw Pyrodigal predictions below 100 aa carry an empirical ~70% false-positive rate if unconstrained. P4's recurrence gate across independent samples ($\ge 3$ samples, $\ge 2$ sites) eliminated 782,616 singleton/doublet clusters, mirroring smORFinder's severe pruning.

### 4. Sommer & Salzberg (2021) — Balrog & Prodigal Error Modes on Contigs
- **Title:** *Balrog: A universal protein model for prokaryotic gene prediction*
- **Authors & Year:** Joshua M. Sommer and Steven L. Salzberg (2021)
- **DOI:** [`10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008727`](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008727)
- **Publisher URL:** [PLOS Computational Biology `10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008727`](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008727)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [PLOS PDF Download](https://journals.plos.org/ploscompbiol/article/file?id=10.1371/journal.pcbi.1008727&type=printable)
- **Local File:** [`sommer_2021_balrog.pdf`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/sommer_2021_balrog.pdf) (1.1 MB PDF)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Prodigal's hexamer and GC-frame training models degrade on fragmented contigs and uncultivated lineages, miscalling translation initiation sites and inflating coding scores on non-coding sequence.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Bears directly on P4's Thermoplasmatota classes (SW-10-69-26 and E2), which are 4–6 times darker than matched bacteria. Because uncultivated archaea lack reference strains, non-canonical start sites or ribosomal motifs may inflate partial gene call rates.

### 5. Dimonaco et al. (2023) — StORF-Reporter & GC-Driven Spurious ORF Physics
- **Title:** *StORF-Reporter: finding genes between genes*
- **Authors & Year:** Nicholas J. Dimonaco, Wayne Aubrey, Kenneth Kenobi, et al. (2023)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/nar/gkad814`](https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkad814)
- **Publisher URL:** [Nucleic Acids Research `10.1093/nar/gkad814`](https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkad814)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC10682499](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC10682499/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`dimonaco_2023_storf-reporter.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/dimonaco_2023_storf-reporter.fulltext.xml) (181.9 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Stop codons (TAA, TAG, TGA) are AT-rich. In high-GC genomes (such as Actinomycetota, 65–75% GC), background stop codon frequency drops >3-fold, causing random non-coding frames to span hundreds of nucleotides before hitting a stop codon, generating long spurious ORFs that mislead predictors.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Actinomycetota represent ~50% of mapped reads and 32% of attributable dark families in P4. High GC content naturally inflates spurious ORF lengths, cautioning that arbitrary length cutoffs cannot alone ensure coding veracity without orthogonal selection tests.

### 6. Davin et al. (2026) — Multi-Omics Reality Check on Metagenomic smORFs
- **Title:** *High-resolution multi-omics enhances prediction and detection of smORF-encoded proteins in the human gut microbiome*
- **Authors & Year:** Marie E. Davin, Joan Ortís Sunyer, Laura F. Delgado, et al. (2026)
- **DOI:** [`10.1038/s41467-026-72762-5`](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-72762-5)
- **Publisher URL:** [Nature Communications `10.1038/s41467-026-72762-5`](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-026-72762-5)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC13376575](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC13376575/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`davin_2026_multiomics-smorf.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/davin_2026_multiomics-smorf.fulltext.xml) (167.3 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Multi-omic benchmarking across metagenomics, metatranscriptomics, Ribo-seq, and mass spectrometry revealed that **>80% of computationally predicted metagenomic smORFs lack detectable peptide or translation evidence**, proving that multi-sample assembly alone does not demonstrate cellular translation.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Challenges any claim equating 106,042 dark families with translated cellular machinery. P4 must explicitly state: the census measures genomic coding capacity and purifying selection, not confirmed cellular translation.

### 7. Liu et al. (2025) — SProtFP: False-Positive Control for smORFs
- **Title:** *SProtFP: a machine learning-based method for functional classification of small ORFs in prokaryotes*
- **Authors & Year:** Yang Liu, Shan Zhang, Xiang Wang, et al. (2025)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/nargab/lqae186`](https://doi.org/10.1093/nargab/lqae186)
- **Publisher URL:** [NAR Genomics and Bioinformatics `10.1093/nargab/lqae186`](https://doi.org/10.1093/nargab/lqae186)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC11704790](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC11704790/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`sprotfp_2025_smorf-classification.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/sprotfp_2025_smorf-classification.fulltext.xml) (166.9 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Single-metric filters fail to control the false-positive rate of prokaryotic smORFs; reliable separation of functional small proteins from non-coding noise requires multi-genome conservation, codon usage bias, and secondary structure potential.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Supports P4's multi-tier evidentiary design: Pyrodigal calls alone are insufficient; independent multi-site recurrence and dN/dS filtering are essential.

### 8. Nekrutenko, Makova & Li (2002) — Foundational Frameshift Null & Ka/Ks Coding Test
- **Title:** *The Ka/Ks ratio test for assessing the protein-coding potential of genomic regions: an empirical and simulation study*
- **Authors & Year:** Anton Nekrutenko, Kateryna D. Makova, Wen-Hsiung Li (2002)
- **DOI:** [`10.1101/gr.200901`](https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.200901)
- **Publisher URL:** [Genome Research `10.1101/gr.200901`](https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.200901)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [PubMed Central PMC155263](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC155263/)
- **Local File:** [`nekrutenko_2002_kaks-coding-potential.fulltext.html`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/nekrutenko_2002_kaks-coding-potential.fulltext.html) (144.7 KB HTML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Established the Ka/Ks test for coding potential: functional coding exons maintain Ka/Ks << 1, whereas non-coding regions and pseudogenes drift around 1. Proved that translating coding sequences out-of-frame abolishes purifying selection, driving Ka/Ks to ~1.0.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Provides foundational validation for P4's +1 frameshifted null arm (median dN/dS = 1.087 shifted vs. 0.427 true frame).

### 9. Nelson, Hughes & Ardern (2020) — OLGenie: Why Frameshifted dN/dS Exceeds 1.0
- **Title:** *OLGenie: Estimating Natural Selection to Predict Functional Overlapping Genes*
- **Authors & Year:** Chase W. Nelson, Zachary Ardern, Austin L. Hughes (2020)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/molbev/msaa087`](https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaa087)
- **Publisher URL:** [Molecular Biology and Evolution `10.1093/molbev/msaa087`](https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msaa087)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC7531306](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC7531306/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`nelson_2020_olgenie-overlapping.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/nelson_2020_olgenie-overlapping.fulltext.xml) (111.9 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** In frame 0, synonymous mutations concentrate at codon position 3. Shifting the frame by +1 nucleotide projects position 3 directly into codon position 2 of the new frame, where **100% of mutations are non-synonymous**. This structural degeneracy projection inflates non-synonymous substitutions, causing out-of-frame dN/dS to naturally exceed 1.0.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Fully explains why P4's shifted null median is 1.087 (not exactly 1.000). It is the expected mathematical artifact of reading an in-frame purifying substitution pattern out-of-frame.

### 10. Yang & Nielsen (2000) — YN00 & The Systematic Biases of NG86
- **Title:** *Estimating Synonymous and Nonsynonymous Substitution Rates Under Realistic Evolutionary Models*
- **Authors & Year:** Ziheng Yang and Rasmus Nielsen (2000)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/oxfordjournals.molbev.a026236`](https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.molbev.a026236)
- **Publisher URL:** [Oxford Academic MBE `10.1093/oxfordjournals.molbev.a026236`](https://doi.org/10.1093/oxfordjournals.molbev.a026236)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** Closed access (paywalled; PAML distribution `abacus.gene.ucl.ac.uk/software/paml.html`).
- **Local File:** Recorded as paywalled in manifest; methodological formulas verified.
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Dissects the systemic flaws of Nei–Gojobori 1986: assuming $\kappa = 1$ (equal transitions/transversions) and uniform codon frequencies ($1/61$) severely underestimates $dS$ and inflates $dN/dS$, especially in high-GC genomes.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Proves that raw NG86 values (0.425) cannot be read as absolute biological constants. P4's length- and size-matched control (0.388) is mandatory because both arms inherit the identical NG86 biases, anchoring the comparison on a shared scale.

### 11. Markova-Raina & Petrov (2011) — Aligner Sensitivity & Selection False Positives
- **Title:** *High sensitivity to aligner and high rate of false positives in the estimates of positive selection in the 12 Drosophila genomes*
- **Authors & Year:** Penka Markova-Raina and Dmitri Petrov (2011)
- **DOI:** [`10.1101/gr.115949.110`](https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.115949.110)
- **Publisher URL:** [Genome Research `10.1101/gr.115949.110`](https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.115949.110)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [PubMed Central PMC3106319](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3106319/)
- **Local File:** [`markova-raina_2011_aligner-sensitivity.fulltext.html`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/markova-raina_2011_aligner-sensitivity.fulltext.html) (234.0 KB HTML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Progressive sequence aligners place gaps arbitrarily near indels. When back-translated to codons, misaligned residues create artificial non-synonymous substitutions, inflating dN and generating false-positive rates of positive selection exceeding 50%.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Explains P4's 3.3% tail of dark families with dN/dS > 1 (vs. 0.4% in control). This tail is enriched in progressive alignment artefacts from MAFFT; P4 is correct to treat it as hypothesis-generating.

### 12. Penn et al. (2010) — GUIDANCE: Alignment Uncertainty in Selection Inference
- **Title:** *An alignment confidence score capturing robustness to guide tree uncertainty*
- **Authors & Year:** Osnat Penn, Eyal Privman, Golan Landan, Dan Graur, Tal Pupko (2010)
- **DOI:** [`10.1093/molbev/msq066`](https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msq066)
- **Publisher URL:** [Molecular Biology and Evolution `10.1093/molbev/msq066`](https://doi.org/10.1093/molbev/msq066)
- **Accessible Full-Text URL:** [Europe PMC PMC2896199](https://www.ebi.ac.uk/europepmc/webservices/rest/PMC2896199/fullTextXML)
- **Local File:** [`penn_2010_guidance.fulltext.xml`](file:///home/leechuck/Documents/papers/empty-quarter-mags/resources/literature/darkmatter-2026-10-13/papers/02_coding_selection/penn_2010_guidance.fulltext.xml) (50.6 KB XML)
- **Already Cited?** No.
- **Precise Claim / Limitation Relevant to P4:** Guide-tree uncertainty in progressive aligners propagates into alignment column errors that corrupt downstream substitution rates. Establishes the necessity of alignment confidence filtering.
- **Challenge to Novelty / Critical Assessment:** Clustering at 50% sequence identity approaches the twilight zone of progressive alignment. P4 mitigated this by requiring translatability without internal stops, stripping gapped positions pairwise, and requiring $\ge 30$ clean codons.

