## 2. Reviewer Attack Surface & Concrete Rebuttal Armor

| Reviewer Attack | Vulnerability in P4 | Literature Counter-Armor & Concrete Defense |
|---|---|---|
| **1. "76% of dark proteins are <100 aa; they are spurious ORFs from MEGAHIT fragmentation."** | Dark catalogue skews heavily toward small proteins; 86.7% are partial calls. | **Cite Miravet-Verde 2019, Durrant 2021, and Dimonaco 2023.** UniRef50 is systematically depleted of smORFs due to historical Prodigal length penalties. P4's recurrence gate ($\ge 3$ samples, $\ge 2$ sites) eliminated 782,616 spurious variants. The cultured genome control (23 species pairs) proves that dark proteins in closed genomes are even shorter (median 59 vs. 70 aa) than in MAGs, demonstrating that assembly fragmentation does not manufacture the short length profile. |
| **2. "Your +1 frameshift null is not an unconstrained neutral control; why is its median 1.087 rather than 1.000?"** | P4 uses a +1 frame-shifted sequence as a null model for selection. | **Cite Nelson et al. 2020 (OLGenie) and Nekrutenko et al. 2002.** Nelson et al. proves mathematically that synonymous mutations in frame 0 (at codon position 3) project into codon position 2 of the +1 frame, where 100% of substitutions are non-synonymous. P4's Methods explicitly states: *"A shifted frame inherits the composition and substitution pattern of the real gene, so this null tests whether the annotated frame is the selected one, not neutral evolution in general."* |
| **3. "Nei-Gojobori 1986 is obsolete; transition bias and high GC content invalidate your dN/dS."** | NG86 assumes $\kappa = 1$ and equal codon frequencies, biasing dN/dS. | **Cite Yang & Nielsen 2000.** Acknowledge NG86 limitations directly. P4 never interprets 0.425 as an uncalibrated absolute parameter; it is calibrated against 106,042 length- and size-matched UniRef50-annotated families (0.388) that share the identical GC composition, alignment, and NG86 counting biases. |
| **4. "Clustering at 50% identity pre-selects conserved sequences, forcing dN/dS below 1."** | 50% identity threshold imposes an ascertainment filter. | **Dossier 02 owns this limitation explicitly:** *"Clustering at 50% identity makes any set of families look conserved, so the selection test runs against a matched annotated control that inherits the same artefact."* The control anchors the scale at 0.388; the +1 frame null on the identical clustered sequences jumps to 1.087, demonstrating that 50% identity clustering alone does not force dN/dS below 1 when the reading frame is disrupted. |
| **5. "Your control skipped the recurrence gate ($\ge 3$ samples, $\ge 2$ sites); your comparison is asymmetric."** | Control admitted on $m \ge 3$ alone, keeping paralog sets dropped from dark arm. | Disclose in Methods M.7 that skipping the sample/site gate on the control is conservative: it retains younger, more divergent within-genome variants that push control dN/dS *higher*, meaning the true gap between dark and control constraint is even smaller than reported. |
| **6. "Metagenomic smORF predictions are non-coding noise that never gets translated."** | P4 measures DNA-level sequencing, not proteomics or translational activity. | **Cite Davin et al. 2026.** Agree explicitly with Davin et al.: computational smORF prediction without metaproteomics cannot assert cellular translation. P4's claims are strictly genomic: dark families represent conserved, persistent coding sequence in the accessory genome of uncultivated lineages. |

